/* client_architecture 11: "Layout is safe-area driven with no fixed pixel
   positions." Nothing here is positioned at a screen coordinate. The margins
   below are GUTTERS inside a root that WaveRunner pads with SafeAreaBinder -
   the same binder BootController applies to the shell.

   VERTICALLY ONLY. SafeAreaBinder sets paddingTop and paddingBottom and
   NOTHING ELSE, so margin-top does measure from the safe area's edge and
   margin-left still measures from the DISPLAY's. An earlier version of this
   comment claimed both. Latent while the project stays portrait-locked, where
   the horizontal inset is zero; the first landscape build on a notched device
   is where it stops being latent.

   The bars ARE absolutely positioned, because they track world bodies; that
   position comes from the camera and is clamped into the frame, never taken
   from a constant.

   THIS IS THE ONE SCREEN THAT IS NOT PAPER-WHITE, and that is deliberate
   rather than an oversight in the design pass. It renders OVER the wave
   scene, not over the shell, so `--paper` here would be a white sheet across
   the battlefield. Light text on the world is correct; what moves onto the
   token palette is the semantic colour of the bars, so a chilled raider is
   the same teal as Vetch everywhere else. bible 10.1 asks for the interface
   to be recessive and the creatures to carry the colour - over live combat
   that argument points this way.

   AND THIS IS WHY IT TAKES NO ScreenScaffold. It is an overlay, not a screen:
   WaveRunner adds it to the wave scene's own `document.rootVisualElement`
   and it never reaches ScreenHost, never enters the back stack, and has no
   header, no CTA row and nothing to scroll. ScaffoldTests exempts it BY NAME
   for that reason, and the exemption is asserted to be exactly two names, so
   giving this file a scaffold to satisfy a sweep - or moving the type out of
   Broodline.UI.Screens to dodge one - would both be working around the test
   rather than with it.

   WHAT PHASE 8 TASK 11 CHANGED, since "no scaffold" is not "no work". Three
   raw colours left this file and the last two bespoke surfaces on it became
   the vocabulary the rest of the app uses:
     - rgba(46, 42, 61, 0.55) on the track is --hud-scrim, which IS --ink-deep
       at 55% and is now a token because the readout wants the same fill. It
       was a raw rgba() sitting in the one file verify-uss-tokens.sh's own
       header names as the reason that gate exists - and rgba() is not hex, so
       check 1 never saw it.
     - rgb(250, 249, 252) and rgb(240, 238, 246), the readout's ink and the
       tag's, are one --surface. They were six levels apart, which is not a
       hierarchy anybody reads; over a scene that changes every frame both
       want the most contrast available, and one ink is one fewer pair to keep
       in step.
     - the readout is a chip rather than bare text, and each bar is wrapped in
       `.elev-1`. Both are the same argument: on --paper a surface can be told
       apart from its background by a hairline, and over a battlefield it
       cannot.

   THIS IS THE ONE STYLESHEET IN TASKS 9-12 THAT GREW - 28 declarations to 32
   - AND THE REASON IS NOT THAT LAYOUT WAS RE-DERIVED. Step 5's rule is a test
   for re-derivation and every one of the five added declarations describes a
   SURFACE THAT DID NOT EXIST: four are the readout chip (align-self, padding,
   fill, radius) written in Theme.uss's own `.panel-*` recipe rather than a
   fourth shape, and one is the elevation wrapper's cancelled top padding. One
   declaration left (the tag's font-size, which `t-num` now carries). Nothing
   here duplicates a class the layer already provides, and the three other
   sheets this task touched went 23 to 9 and 31 to 10.

   THE WAVE NUMBER ARRIVED IN PHASE 9 TASK 18 AND `HudSnapshot` DID NOT
   CHANGE. This note used to record the counter as owed, and it named the
   right obstacle for the wrong route: `HudSnapshot` does carry Integrity,
   Tick and Bodies and nothing else (Model/WaveReport.cs:146-158), and adding
   a fourth field would have reached Broodline.Model, WaveRunner's per-frame
   snapshot builder and `WaveHudScreen.Integrity`'s sentence - which the
   device re-capture procedure reads off the screen to time a tap. None of
   that was necessary. The wave id is not per-frame data: it is fixed for the
   whole run, `WaveRunner.Configure` is handed the `WaveDef` that carries it,
   and `WaveHudView.Wave` is a property set once at attach, on the precedent
   `Camera` already set on the same view ("not a `Bind` parameter: the brief
   pins Bind(Func<HudSnapshot>) and the camera outlives any one binding").

   WHAT IS STILL NOT DRAWN, AND IT IS THE REST OF THE HANDOFF'S TOP ROW.
   `Wave Defense.dc.html:35` is a pause tile and `:42` a speed pill, one each
   side of the wave counter, and neither is here. Three measurements, each on
   its own sufficient: there is no `icon--pause` and no `icon--speed` in
   `icons.uss` (sixteen `.icon--*` rules, none of them either); every element in this
   view is `PickingMode.Ignore` and `WaveScreensTests
   .WaveHud_AcceptsNoPointerEventAnywhere` sweeps the whole subtree to keep it
   that way; and `WaveRunner.Update` makes a tap ANYWHERE Rally creature 0,
   which combat_engine section 8 makes the player's only input during a wave.
   So a pause glyph here would not be an inert control - it would be a control
   whose tap does something else, which is strictly worse than not drawing it.
   `WaveDefeatView.Bind` already states the rule this follows: a control that
   does nothing reads as an app that is broken.

   AND THE LEFT STAT PILL - `Gene energy`, `:54` - IS NOT DRAWN EITHER.
   `WaveHudScreen.IntegrityLabel`'s comment has that one: there is no energy
   in this game's combat loop for it to state. */
.wave-hud-view {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-direction: column;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------- the chrome */

/* THE GUTTER LIVES HERE NOW, ONCE, FOR BOTH THE BAND AND THE READOUT. Each
 * used to carry its own `margin-left: var(--gutter)`; `Wave Defense.dc.html`
 * puts both inside the 12px page gutter at `:48` (`:34` is 14, not 12) and they are
 * one block of chrome, so one element owns it.
 *
 * `align-items: flex-start` RATHER THAN A `flex-start` ON EACH CHILD, which
 * is the same shrink-wrap the readout has always had, hoisted. The root is a
 * column, so a child stretches the full width by default - and a full-width
 * scrim under one short line is a strip across the top of the battlefield,
 * not a readout. It is also what keeps the band `ClampIntoFrame` reserves the
 * width of the thing actually being avoided.
 *
 * NOT `position: absolute`. It is the first child of a column whose only
 * other child is the absolutely-positioned bar layer, so the chrome's own
 * height is what `Place` measures and the bars are laid over it. */
.wave-hud-view__chrome {
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-left: var(--gutter);
    margin-right: var(--gutter);
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
}

/* THE TOP BAND, IN Theme.uss's `.panel-*` RECIPE AND NOT A FOURTH SHAPE -
 * fill, --radius-row, one padding step - because the five panel classes are
 * what a tinted surface looks like everywhere else in the app. It is not one
 * of those classes only because none of them is scrim: every `.panel-*` is a
 * light tint for ink on paper, and this one carries white ink over a
 * battlefield. --space-2 rather than --space-3 because this is chrome over a
 * scene and not a block on a page; both are on the scale. */
.wave-hud-view__band {
    background-color: var(--hud-frame);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--brass);
    border-radius: var(--radius-row);
    padding: var(--space-2);
}

/* `Wave Defense.dc.html:39` - the handoff's `.lbl`, in the ONE ink this file
 * carries. Its own header states that ruling: over a scene that changes every
 * frame every readout wants the most contrast available, and one ink is one
 * fewer pair to keep in step. The handoff's #a29bb5 is --mute, which is a mid
 * grey and is the right answer on paper and the wrong one on a scrim.
 *
 * TWO CLASSES, to beat `.t-micro`'s single-class --mute however the panel
 * orders the two sheets - the rule every override of a Theme class in this
 * phase follows. */
.wave-hud-view .wave-hud-view__eyebrow {
    color: var(--surface);
    height: 14px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.wave-hud-view__wave-line {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-top: 1px;
}

/* `:40` draws "Wave {{ wave }}" as one 20px Baloo line and this is two Labels
 * on one row, for the reason `DeployView`'s title line is: bible 10.6 keeps
 * numerals out of the display face and puts `t-num` on every decision-bearing
 * one, so the word and the number cannot be one run of text.
 * `align-items: center` rather than the baseline the handoff gets for free -
 * UI Toolkit has no `align-items: baseline` at all.
 *
 * --text-screen-title IS 21 AGAINST THE HANDOFF'S 20, which is the nearest
 * step on the scale and the size the deploy screen already draws the same
 * words at, 30 seconds earlier in the same beat. */
.wave-hud-view .wave-hud-view__wave-word {
    color: var(--surface);
    height: 29px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* THE NUMBERS AT THE WORD'S OWN SIZE, which `.t-num` would otherwise take to
 * --text-secondary (11px) - half the height of the word beside it. This is
 * the fourth place in the project to raise a `.t-num` label above the 11px
 * floor, after `.resource-pill__value` (15), `.cost-cta-row__cost` (17) and
 * `.deploy-view__wave-number` (21); the floor is a MINIMUM, so going up does
 * not touch bible 10.6, and what it forbids is --text-micro, which
 * verify-uss-tokens.sh check 2 reads.
 *
 * 4px, WHICH IS THE SPACE CHARACTER THE HANDOFF HAS AND THIS ROW DOES NOT:
 * two Labels have no word gap between them. --space-1 at this size reads as
 * the one space in "Wave 6". */
.wave-hud-view .wave-hud-view__wave-number {
    font-size: var(--text-screen-title);
    color: var(--surface);
    height: 29px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    margin-left: var(--space-1);
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------- the stat readout */

/* `:48` - `display: flex; gap: 7px; padding: 0 12px 10px`. The 12px is the
 * chrome's gutter above and is not restated; the 10px below it is the gap to
 * the lane, which here is the battlefield itself and needs none. 6px is the
 * handoff's own distance between the band's bottom edge and the readouts'
 * top, measured on `specs/Designs/shots/wave-defense.png` at 2x. */
.wave-hud-view__pills {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 6px;
}

/* ONE PILL, NOT THE HANDOFF'S TWO - see `WaveHudScreen.IntegrityLabel`.
 * Everything else about it is `:58`'s: a rounded box with a 22px tinted disc
 * at the left and a two-line column beside it. The fill is the scrim rather
 * than the handoff's #ffffff, for this file's own reason: a white pill is a
 * white card over a battlefield. */
.wave-hud-view__pill {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    background-color: var(--hud-frame);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--brass);
    border-radius: var(--radius-row);
    padding-top: var(--space-2);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
    padding-left: 11px;
    padding-right: 11px;
}

/* `:59` - a 22px circle behind an 11px glyph, the same ornament
 * `.deploy-view__pill-disc` draws on the placing phase of this screen. 11 is
 * half of 22, so this is a true circle: a square element clamps both axes
 * alike, which is the one shape immune to the lens defect Theme.uss's header
 * item 4 measured. Not shared with the deploy screen's rule because a screen's
 * stylesheet reaches its own descendants and this disc is not one of them. */
.wave-hud-view__pill-disc {
    width: 22px;
    height: 22px;
    border-radius: 11px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-right: var(--space-2);
    background-color: var(--coral-tint);
}

/* TWO CLASSES, to beat `icons.uss`'s 19px and --mute-soft without depending on
 * which sheet the panel applies last. The handoff draws the mark at 11px
 * inside the 22px disc and strokes it #c96555; --coral-deep (#d4776a) is the
 * nearest token at 11+18+21 = 50 in summed channel distance and is the same
 * pairing `.deploy-view__pill-disc--integrity` already makes with the same
 * tint, so the two phases of this screen draw one ornament. */
.wave-hud-view .wave-hud-view__pill-glyph {
    width: 11px;
    height: 11px;
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: var(--coral-deep);
}

/* HEIGHT ON THE BOX, NEVER ON THE LABEL - Phase 9 Task 16b's rule. Every
 * Label in this file's chrome carries one, including the three in the band
 * above: measured on `WaveDefeatView.png`, a Label that is a CHILD of a column
 * reserves one extra line box of leading that nothing declares, and 29 is
 * --text-screen-title's own (21 x 1.36 = 28.6, rounded up). These two are
 * instances of the shape it was written for: Labels
 * that are CHILDREN of a column rather than the box itself, which 16b measured
 * at 37-38px inside a 20px chip. The numbers are the authored line boxes
 * rounded up - Nunito-Bold SDF is m_LineHeight 122.76 on m_PointSize 90 =
 * 1.36em (`Nunito-Bold SDF.asset:149,152`), so 10px x 1.36 = 13.6 -> 14 and
 * 17px x 1.36 = 23.1 -> 24. Up rather than down, because a height UNDER the
 * line box clips descenders and there are none to spare at 10px.
 * `-unity-text-align: middle-left` is the other half of the rule: a height on
 * its own pins the box and leaves the glyphs hanging from its top edge.
 * `.deploy-view__pill-label` and `-value` are the same pair on the same
 * handoff element in its other phase. */
.wave-hud-view .wave-hud-view__pill-label {
    height: 14px;
    color: var(--surface);
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* bible 10.6's 11px is a FLOOR, not a target, and --text-secondary sits
 * exactly on it. This is the single number a player reads mid-wave, at arm's
 * length, with a thumb somewhere over the screen, against a scene that is
 * moving - so it takes the largest size on the scale that still reads as
 * chrome rather than as a headline. It was a bespoke 15px before Phase 8 Task
 * 11, which is on no scale at all; 17px is --text-cta, the project's own
 * "read this at a glance" size. */
.wave-hud-view .wave-hud-view__integrity {
    font-size: var(--text-cta);
    height: 24px;
    color: var(--surface);
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* THE LIVE TICK, AND IT IS DRESSED AS AN INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN AS A STAT -
 * Phase 9 Task 21e. It used to be appended to `.wave-hud-view__integrity`,
 * which put a frame counter inside the VALUE of a pill captioned "ARK
 * INTEGRITY" and read, to the person who walked the packaged app, as
 * "2 tick 155".
 *
 * UNDER THE PILL, NOT IN ONE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT. The pill is the
 * handoff's stat anatomy - tinted disc, caption, value - and anything inside
 * it claims to BE the stat. Outside it, in its own small scrim, at a third of
 * the value's size, it is plainly a separate readout.
 * `WaveHudScreen.Tick` has why it is on screen at all.
 *
 * ITS OWN SCRIM, BECAUSE IT IS NOT INSIDE ANYTHING ELSE'S. Both other pieces
 * of chrome on this screen carry white ink on --hud-scrim - the band at :129
 * and the pill at :208 - and they can, because each is a filled box. This
 * Label is a direct child of `#chrome`, so with no fill of its own it would
 * be white text laid straight on a pale-green battlefield. Same recipe as the
 * band, one step smaller in padding because it holds one short line rather
 * than a two-line column, and `align-items: flex-start` on `#chrome` already
 * shrink-wraps it.
 *
 * --text-secondary IS `t-num`'s OWN SIZE AND IS NOT RESTATED HERE. 11px is
 * bible 10.6's floor, and the tick IS a number a decision depends on - the
 * capturer times a tap by it - so it sits ON the floor rather than under it:
 * the smallest thing this file is allowed to draw, which is the right size
 * for the least important number on the screen. `t-num` also brings the
 * tabular digits, without which a counter advancing thirty times a second
 * would jitter as its glyph widths changed.
 *
 * HEIGHT ON THE BOX WITH `middle-left`, THIS FILE'S OWN RULE - see
 * `.wave-hud-view__pill-label`'s note four rules up, which has the
 * measurement. 11px x 1.36 = 14.96 -> 15, rounded UP because a height under
 * the line box clips descenders.
 *
 * 6px MATCHES `.wave-hud-view__pills`' OWN TOP MARGIN, which is the handoff's
 * measured distance between two stacked chrome rows on this screen. */
.wave-hud-view .wave-hud-view__tick {
    height: 15px;
    color: var(--surface);
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    background-color: var(--hud-scrim);
    border-radius: var(--radius-row);
    padding-top: var(--space-1);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-1);
    padding-left: var(--space-2);
    padding-right: var(--space-2);
    margin-top: 6px;
}

.wave-hud-view__bars {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
}

.wave-hud-view__bar {
    position: absolute;
    width: 52px;
    flex-direction: column;
}

/* The elevation wrapper, in SectionCard's exact arrangement: `.elev-1` on a
 * wrapper that is padding plus the nine-sliced shadow, with the opaque
 * surface as its child. Theme.uss header note 2 has why UI Toolkit leaves no
 * other option, and the negative horizontal margin 615229c added is what puts
 * the track back on the bar's own 52px rather than 6px inside it: the wrapper
 * stretches to 64, pads 6 a side, and the track comes out 52 and centred on
 * the body it labels. Measured on the capture, row y=3: the track runs
 * x=0..51 and the ring's ink x=52..55 at 12, 8, 4 and 4 levels before paper
 * at x=56 - so `BarWidth` is unchanged and the centring `Place` does is still
 * against the thing a player sees.
 *
 * THE TOP PADDING IS CANCELLED AND THE BOTTOM IS NOT, WHICH IS THE VERTICAL
 * CASE Theme.uss's "horizontal only" DECISION EXPLICITLY SCOPED AROUND. That
 * decision is about two STACKED CARDS - cancelling vertically there would put
 * one card's white edge inside the other's shadow, and there is nothing
 * stacked here. What there is instead is a position computed from the camera:
 * `Place` positions the bar ROOT, and 6px of padding above the track moves the
 * track 6px down the body it labels. Measured before this line: the fill ran
 * y=6..11 where the previous capture had it at y=0..5. The lift is the one
 * thing this view's class comment records three failed derivations of, so 6px
 * of it is not given away to a shadow ring. The BOTTOM padding stays - that is
 * where the drop actually falls, and it is also the gap to the tag.
 *
 * ONE DECLARATION, because `flex-direction: column` is Yoga's default and
 * writing it out would be this file re-deriving the layer rather than
 * composing it - which is the thing step 5 of this task is a test for. */
.wave-hud-view__elev {
    margin-top: -6px;
}

.wave-hud-view__track {
    height: 6px;
    flex-direction: row;
    background-color: var(--hud-scrim);
    border-radius: 3px;
}

/* Default is a raider: coral, the token table's danger. */
.wave-hud-view__fill {
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background-color: var(--coral);
}

/* Yours. Green - the same success colour the post-wave screen uses. */
.wave-hud-view__bar.creature .wave-hud-view__fill { background-color: var(--green); }

/* Chill applied: teal, matching the Vetch/info token. Chill is the trait the
 * wave-6 defeat screen teaches, so it should be the same colour there and
 * here or the lesson does not transfer. */
.wave-hud-view__bar.chilled .wave-hud-view__fill { background-color: var(--teal); }

/* Rally: amber-bright, the apex/attention colour. */
.wave-hud-view__bar.rallied .wave-hud-view__fill { background-color: var(--amber-bright); }

/* Breaching - the one state a player must catch in peripheral vision, so it
 * is the brightest thing on the field. */
.wave-hud-view__bar.breaching .wave-hud-view__fill { background-color: var(--coral-alert); }

/* Centred under the track it belongs to, rather than left-aligned under a
 * 52px column, so a three-letter tag and a seven-letter one both sit over the
 * body they label.
 *
 * ITS SIZE IS NOT SET HERE ANY MORE. "RALLY 42" ends in a countdown, and
 * combat_engine section 8 makes Rally the player's ONLY input during a wave -
 * so that remainder is a number a decision depends on, which is bible 10.6's
 * own test for the `t-num` marker. The class is on the element in `NewBar`
 * and it already carries --text-secondary, the exact 11px this rule used to
 * write out. Same pixels, one fewer place for the floor to be lost. */
.wave-hud-view__tag {
    -unity-text-align: upper-center;
    color: var(--surface);
}

/* THE TWO CONTROLS - Phase 10 Task 1.7. Slate pills on the brass-edged
 * frame's vocabulary; no foot, because a 28px pill that drops 2px reads as
 * a glitch rather than a press. */
.wave-hud-view__controls {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
}
.wave-hud-view .wave-hud-view__control {
    background-color: var(--hud-frame);
    color: var(--surface-paper);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--brass);
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: var(--brass);
    border-radius: 14px;
    padding-top: 5px;
    padding-bottom: 5px;
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    padding-right: var(--space-3);
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-right: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--text-body);
}
.wave-hud-view .wave-hud-view__control:active {
    translate: 0 0;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    background-color: var(--hud-frame-edge);
}
